aria: Heads-up: a partner reported our SDK inflates Vercel edge bundles because `undici` sneaks in. We need a super short, copyable section for README covering ESM/CJS, edge-friendliness, and a TS typing fallback.

pete: Core rule: never statically import undici at top-level. Provide a small runtime fetch resolver that prefers an injected fetch, then globalThis.fetch, then dynamic import('undici'). Example to paste: 
```js
// fetch-resolver.js
export function resolver(provided) {
  return async function fetcher(input, init) {
    if (provided) return provided(input, init);
    if (globalThis.fetch) return globalThis.fetch(input, init);
    try { const { fetch } = await import('undici'); return fetch(input, init); }
    catch (err) { throw new Error('No fetch available: install undici or pass a fetch implementation'); }
  }
}
```
This avoids bundling undici into edge/worker builds because bundlers prefer the edge entrypoint and the shim doesn't reference undici.

nina: Packaging reminders: publish both an ESM and CJS build and use conditional exports. Provide a tiny `fetch-shim` entry that only re-exports globalThis.fetch so bundlers targeting the web/edge pick that file instead. Mark undici as optionalDependency so installs don't always include it. Example package.json snippet to include in README: 
```json
"exports": {
  ".": { "import": "./dist/index.mjs", "require": "./dist/index.cjs" },
  "./fetch-shim": { "import": "./dist/fetch-shim.mjs", "require": "./dist/fetch-shim.cjs" }
},
"optionalDependencies": { "undici": "^5.0.0" }
```

aria: TS types are a recurring support headache. Many Node-only projects don't include DOM lib and get `RequestInit`/`Response` missing. Quick options: (A) document that consumers add "DOM" to tsconfig.lib for full types; (B) ship tiny ambient types under `types/minifetch.d.ts` that export a minimal FetchLike type; (C) provide a typed factory that accepts a generic Fetch type to avoid leaking DOM types in SDK surface.

pete: Minimal type file suggestion (pasteable): 
```ts
// types/minifetch.d.ts
export type FetchLike = (input: string | URL, init?: any) => Promise<any>;
export default FetchLike;
```
Point package.json `types` field at the typed entry or include it under `exports` so TS picks it up. This avoids forcing DOM lib for downstream projects.

nina: CI: add a lightweight artifact scan job that `npm pack`s the edge build and `tar -tf` the package; fail if `node_modules/undici` or `undici/` files show up. Also smoke-test in node:14/16/18 matrix plus run a Cloudflare worker smoke test (wrangler or a minimal worker runtime) to ensure global fetch path works.

ops-bot: CI update: added job "edge-undici-scan" to PR checks.

aria: One tiny gotcha — some bundlers will inline dynamic import targets if you use string concatenation or if lockfiles force optional deps to be present during pack. Recommend docs: "If you still see undici in edge bundles, check package-lock for optionalDependencies and ensure your bundler's resolve prefers `exports`/`browser` fields."

pete: For CJS consumers: note that the resolver is async. If someone does `const { resolver } = require('sdk')` and then calls it, they must await the returned fetcher. Add a one-line CJS note in README: "CJS: await fetcher(...)."

aria: Great — I'll add a short three-recipe README box: (1) Edge — use ESM entrypoint and no undici, (2) Node 18+ — no action, global fetch used, (3) Node 14/16 — install undici or pass a fetch. I'll open a PR with the resolver, minifetch types, package.json snippets, and CI job reference.

nina: LGTM. I'll also add a short troubleshooting FAQ: how to check your bundle, npm pack inspection commands, and the exact runtime error users will see if fetch is missing.

aria: Thanks team :thumbsup:
